\section{Per-Turn AR GAR Dynamics}\label{sec:appendix-ar-gar-dynamics}

To characterize how goal attention evolves across conversation turns under autoregressive generation, we compute per-turn AR GAR for all six Qwen-2.5-7B training configurations (Base, LoRA-only, Uniform Gate, PCGrad, Scheduled Sampling, ThreadGate) on the held-out evaluation set. Figure~\ref{fig:kl_ar_gar_curves} plots per-turn AR GAR trajectories across turns 1--5, and Figure~\ref{fig:kl_ar_gar_bar} compares mid-turn AR GAR levels across configurations.

All six configurations exhibit monotonic GAR decay as turn index increases, consistent with cumulative exposure bias eroding attention to goal tokens. The decay rate, however, varies substantially across training strategies. ThreadGate and Scheduled Sampling retain the highest mid-turn GAR, followed by PCGrad, Uniform Gate, LoRA-only, and Base (in decreasing order). Base and LoRA-only decay most rapidly, losing the majority of their initial GAR within the first few turns. ThreadGate and Scheduled Sampling maintain comparatively higher GAR through turn 5, suggesting that both turn-conditioned gating and distributional robustification provide partial protection against attention drift.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig_kl_ar_gar_curves.pdf}
\caption{Per-turn AR GAR trajectories for six Qwen-2.5-7B configurations (turns 1--5). All models show monotonic decay; ThreadGate and Scheduled Sampling decay slowest, while Base and LoRA-only decay fastest.}
\label{fig:kl_ar_gar_curves}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig_kl_ar_gar_bar.pdf}
\caption{Mid-turn AR GAR comparison across six training configurations. Ranking from highest to lowest: ThreadGate, Scheduled Sampling, PCGrad, Uniform Gate, LoRA-only, Base.}
\label{fig:kl_ar_gar_bar}
\end{figure}

This pattern contrasts with teacher-forced evaluation, where GAR remains relatively stable across turns for all configurations. Under teacher forcing, the model conditions on ground-truth context at each turn, preventing the distributional drift that causes GAR erosion in AR mode. The divergence between TF stability and AR decay provides turn-level evidence for the exposure bias mechanism described in \S\ref{sec:analysis}: training-time attention patterns are optimized for a distribution that AR generation progressively departs from.

